<title>USA: RAINS IMPROVE GEORGIA COTTON RATINGS IN WEEK.</title>
<headline>RAINS IMPROVE GEORGIA COTTON RATINGS IN WEEK.</headline>
<dateline>ATHENS 1997-08-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>The following is the weekly crop update from the Georgia Agricultural Statistics Service for the week ended Sunday, August 10.</p>
<p>--</p>
<p>CROP HIGHLIGHTS</p>
<p>It was another week of scattered rainfall throughout the State, according to the Georgia Agricultural Statistics Service. The moisture was generally adequate for crop development and interfered little with field work except in a few isolated areas. Generally, crops are in fair to mostly good condition and little changed from last week.</p>
<p>Cotton conditions improved some during the week. However, pressure from worms is intensifying and spraying is active. Insect control is now increasing in the northern cotton fields with reports of heavy infestations continuing in many southern regions. Cotton development is about a week behind normal. The peanut crop improved slightly from last week. Peanuts pegging is still later than usual. Farmers continue to apply fungicides to control disease problems.</p>
<p>Crop conditions for corn held steady for the week. Corn development and harvest are ahead of last year's progress, but are several days behind the five year average. Soybean conditions improved and development is several days ahead of normal.</p>
<p>Tobacco harvest is almost a week ahead of normal. Conditions changed little and the crop remaining for harvest is rated mostly fair to good. The crop ratings for watermelons improved and most of the crop left to be harvested is rated as good. Apple conditions are mostly good, with harvest ahead of last year's pace and nearly equal to normal. Peach harvest is in the final stages.  Pastures and hay fields are in mostly fair to good condition and unchanged from last week. Hay harvest was delayed in some areas by showers.</p>
<p>County Extension Officials reported 6.0 days suitable for fieldwork during the week. Other activities included cutting and baling hay, harvesting corn silage, scouting cotton, applying insecticides to soybeans, and planting fall vegetables.</p>
